        <div class="fc-delta-reason">Only ~6 vessels/day transiting (vs ~120 baseline), representing ~5% of normal traffic. Lloyd's war-risk premiums at 8x pre-crisis levels — well above the 1.5% threshold indicator. No Maersk/MSC/Frontline return announcement. Iran's five-precondition demand includes Hormuz sovereignty recognition — a demand the US is unlikely to accept — creating structural blockage of any commercial reopening. Iran allowing selective passage for Chinese vessels (NBC report) does not meet the 50-vessel threshold or the Western-insurer criterion per the ambiguity rule.</div>
        <dl class="fc-meta-row">
          <dt>Resolution</dt><dd>YES if Hormuz Strait Monitor (or UNCTAD, or Lloyd's List) reports ≥50 daily transits averaged over 7 consecutive days within the horizon window.</dd>
          <dt>Sources OK</dt><dd>Hormuz Strait Monitor (primary), Lloyd's List Intelligence (secondary), UNCTAD Maritime Trade quarterly report (lagging confirmation).</dd>
          <dt>Ambiguity</dt><dd>Only Iranian-flagged or dark-fleet vessels make up the count but Western insurers still refuse = AMBIGUOUS-RESOLVE-AS-NO.</dd>
        </dl>
