  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Tasnim News Agency — editorially aligned with the IRGC — ran Baghaei's "14-point plan exclusively focused on ending the war" formulation as the dominant framing in the May 12-13 window, distinguishing Iran's counter-proposal from the US nuclear demand. The IRGC-channel framing treats the 14-point document as Iran's creation, not a US framework under consideration — a framing distinction that mattered for <span class="cite">F1</span>'s resolution. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span></p>

      <p>Press TV and IRIB described the US draft as "meaning Iran's surrender to Trump's excessive demands," with IRIB deploying that exact phrase by May 11. This is the standard state-broadcaster register for a document the regime has not rejected but wants the domestic audience to see as resisted. The absence of Kayhan's typically sharper rejection-grade language ("mardud," "rad shod") is the operative gap: Kayhan ran editorials critical of the US position but did not use the formal rejection vocabulary about the 14-point framework specifically. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span> without move</p>

      <p>Former IRGC commander Jafari's May 12 statement that "no negotiations" would proceed without preconditions was carried on semi-official channels including Tasnim but not attributed to the Foreign Ministry or Khamenei's office. Araghchi's travel to New Delhi for the BRICS conclave was covered neutrally by IRNA, without the dismissive framing that would accompany a genuine diplomatic walkout. Pezeshkian's May 11 statement — carried in full on IRNA — used "dialogue" and "negotiation" language explicitly, which in Iranian state-media grammar is a permission signal for continued diplomatic contact at the institutional level. → supports <span class="cite">F2</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

